// CCAR-F — Claude Certified Architect Foundations practice question bank.
// Independent, unofficial practice content. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Anthropic.
// Blueprint figures from Anthropic's published Exam Guide v1.0 (July 2026).
// Blueprint and product facts last verified 2026-08-07; see PROVENANCE.md.
//
// ---- Diversity pass ----
// An external review found eleven clusters of items that restated one lesson in different
// scenario clothing. Because a draw spans four scenario blocks, a candidate could meet the
// same reasoning and the same key several times in one exam. Twelve items were rewritten
// onto mechanics the bank did not previously cover: prompt caching and stable prefixes
// (Q40), untrusted retrieved content and indirect prompt injection (Q72), session resume
// after interruption (Q80), run-level tool-call/time/cost budgets (Q103), configuration
// precedence between personal and committed settings (Q117), separating the agent's
// classification from the pipeline's enforcement policy (Q119), MCP tools vs resources vs
// prompts (Q123), pre-declared permissions for unattended runs (Q127), false positives vs
// false negatives when tuning a gate (Q130), and handling a violated output contract at a
// branch point (Q132). Each replacement kept its predecessor's domain, type and scenario,
// so the blueprint apportionment and the two-multi-select-per-scenario shape are unchanged.
//
// Clusters deliberately left at two items: same-inputs comparison (Q43/Q115), optional
// fields (Q138/Q156), schema-vs-semantics (Q130 replaced, Q137 kept), and grep-vs-glob
// (Q38/Q159). A pair testing one principle in two settings is transfer practice, not
// redundancy, and the reviewer's own guidance was to preserve operational Claude Code
// coverage rather than strip it. Q38 and Q159 also test genuinely different tools —
// content search against filename matching — not one lesson twice.
//
// Second pass: the tool-scoping quartet flagged above was cut to two. Q63 (how many tools
// per subagent) and Q85 (scoping access by team) were kept; Q94 became tool-name collision
// across servers, and Q134 became tool granularity — one parameterised tool against several
// narrow ones. Both replacements keep R2, single-select and their original scenario.

registerCourse({
  code: "CCAR-F",
  name: "Claude Certified Architect",
  tier: "Foundations",
  blurb: "Scenario-based architecture judgement: agentic loops and orchestration, tool and MCP design, Claude Code configuration, prompting, and context reliability.",
  audience: "Solution architects with hands-on experience designing systems on Claude and the Anthropic API.",
  items: 60,
  minutes: 120,
  passScore: 720,
  fee: "$125 USD",
  domains: [
    { id: "R1", name: "Agentic Architecture", weight: 27.0 },
    { id: "R3", name: "Claude Code & Workflows", weight: 20.0 },
    { id: "R4", name: "Prompt Engineering & Output", weight: 20.0 },
    { id: "R2", name: "Tool Design & MCP", weight: 18.0 },
    { id: "R5", name: "Context & Reliability", weight: 15.0 },
  ],
  scenarioDraw: { scenarios: 4, perScenario: 15 },
  scenarios: [
    { id: "support", title: "Customer Support Resolution Agent", text: "You are building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK. It handles high-ambiguity requests such as returns, billing disputes, and account changes. It has tools for order lookup, refund issuing, and ticket escalation, and it reads from a policy knowledge base. Conversations can run long, and some cases must reach a human." },
    { id: "codegen", title: "Code Generation with Claude Code", text: "Your team uses Claude Code for generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation across a large shared repository. Engineers work in different subdirectories with different conventions. You are responsible for configuring it so the whole team gets consistent behaviour, and for deciding what belongs in shared configuration versus each developer's own setup." },
    { id: "research", title: "Multi-Agent Research System", text: "You are building a multi-agent research system on the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent receives a research question, decomposes it, and delegates to subagents that search and read sources. Subagents return findings the coordinator synthesises into a report. Runs are long, sources are numerous, and the report must be traceable back to what was actually found." },
    { id: "devtools", title: "Developer Productivity Tooling", text: "You are building internal tooling on the Claude Agent SDK to help engineers explore unfamiliar codebases, understand legacy systems, and answer questions about internal services. It connects to several internal systems through MCP servers, and different teams need access to different subsets of those systems." },
    { id: "cicd", title: "Claude Code in a CI/CD Pipeline", text: "You are integrating Claude Code into a CI/CD pipeline that runs automated code review on pull requests, generates release notes, and triages failing builds. It runs unattended with no interactive terminal, its output is consumed by other pipeline steps, and a failure must not silently block the team's merges." },
    { id: "extract", title: "Structured Data Extraction", text: "You are building a system that extracts structured information from unstructured documents — invoices, contracts, and scanned forms — validates it against a schema, and writes it to a downstream database. Documents vary in quality and completeness, and incorrect data reaching the database is expensive to unwind." },
  ],
  questions: [
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent sometimes keeps working after the user's problem is resolved. What should determine when its loop ends?",o:["A phrase in the response text indicating the work is finished","A timer that ends the loop after a set number of seconds","A fixed cap on how many iterations the loop may run for","The stop reason returned with the model's response"],c:[3],e:"Loop termination should be driven by the structured stop reason. Parsing natural language for completion signals is brittle, and caps and timers are safety nets rather than the primary mechanism.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A refund tool must never run before an eligibility check. What is the reliable way to enforce that order?",o:["A tool description on the refund tool noting the check comes first","An example in the prompt showing the correct order being followed","A programmatic hook that blocks the refund call until the check has run","A system prompt instruction stating the required order of the two tools"],c:[2],e:"Financial ordering constraints need programmatic enforcement. Prompt instructions, descriptions, and examples all have a non-zero failure rate that a business rule cannot tolerate.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A customer's request falls outside every approved policy the agent has been given. What should happen?",o:["Escalate to a human with the conversation and the policies checked","Answer from general knowledge, noting that the reply is an unofficial one","Ask the customer to rephrase until the request matches a known policy","Decline the request, since no approved policy authorises an answer"],c:[0],e:"A policy gap is a checkable trigger, and the human needs the conversation plus what was already ruled out. Answering unofficially, stalling the customer, or refusing outright each leave a legitimate request unresolved.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Long conversations are summarised to save context, and order numbers are being lost. What is the fix?",o:["Summarise less aggressively so more of the original text survives","Hold transactional details in a facts block that is never summarised","Ask the model to preserve any numbers it encounters when summarising","Move to a model with a larger context window so summarising is rarer"],c:[1],e:"Progressive summarisation paraphrases exact values away. A verbatim facts block carried in every prompt is what protects transactional specifics.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The agent frequently calls the ticket escalation tool when it should call order lookup. What should be fixed first?",o:["The two tool descriptions, so their boundaries are unambiguous","The system prompt, by adding rules about which tool to prefer","The tool set, by merging the two tools into a single broader one","The model tier, by moving to one better at selecting between tools"],c:[0],e:"Misrouting between similar tools is a description problem first. Clarifying what each does and where the boundary lies precedes prompt rules or restructuring.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"What should a strong description for the refund tool specify?",o:["A warning that refunds are a sensitive financial operation","What it does, its expected inputs, and when not to use it","The internal service it calls and the team that maintains it","The average time the underlying refund operation takes to run"],c:[1],e:"Purpose, inputs, and explicit boundaries against similar tools are what drive correct selection. Implementation detail and generic warnings don't help the model choose.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The model returns a refusal partway through an agent loop rather than a tool call. What should the loop do?",o:["Retry the same turn repeatedly until the model returns a usable tool call","Stop the loop and surface the refusal, rather than re-prompting for a tool call","Treat it as a completed turn and move straight on to the next planned step","Substitute a default tool call so the loop can continue to completion"],c:[1],e:"A refusal is a terminal stop reason for that turn and should be surfaced. Re-prompting or synthesising a call papers over a decision the model made deliberately.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An order lookup fails and the customer's question cannot be fully answered. What should the agent's reply do?",o:["Hand the conversation to a human without attempting a partial answer","Keep retrying the lookup in the background before replying at all","Give its best estimate of the order details rather than admitting a gap","Answer what it can and state plainly which part it could not retrieve"],c:[3],e:"Partial service with the gap named is more useful than silence, a premature handoff, or an invented figure the customer may act on.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Which part of the agent should own the decision to escalate?",o:["The agent loop, using defined criteria checked on each turn","The refund tool, which can escalate when a case looks unusual","The customer, who can request a human at any point in the conversation","The knowledge base, which flags topics that require human handling"],c:[0],e:"Escalation is a control-flow decision belonging to the loop against explicit criteria. A customer request is one such criterion but not the whole mechanism.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The agent gives inconsistent answers to the same policy question across conversations. What is the most likely cause?",o:["The model is inherently inconsistent on questions of this kind","The policy knowledge base contains too few documents overall","The relevant policy is not being reliably retrieved into context","The conversations are too short for the policy to take effect"],c:[2],e:"Inconsistency on a documented policy usually means retrieval isn't reliably surfacing the governing passage, so the model is answering from general knowledge instead.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A new tool is needed to check warranty status against a third-party system. What should be evaluated first?",o:["Which model tier will call the new tool most reliably","Whether an existing MCP server already exposes that system","How the new tool's description ought to be worded for the model","Which transport the new custom server ought to use"],c:[1],e:"Checking for an existing server before building one avoids duplicating an integration that may already be maintained elsewhere.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent occasionally issues a refund larger than the original order. What control addresses this most directly?",o:["Validation inside the refund tool that rejects amounts above the order total","A note in the tool's description about the maximum refund permitted","A review of refunds above the order total after they have been issued","A prompt instruction never to refund more than the original order was worth"],c:[0],e:"A hard constraint belongs in the tool's own validation, where it cannot be talked around. Prompt-level rules and after-the-fact review both allow the bad call through.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Key case details appear in the middle of a very long prompt and are being missed. What is the practical fix?",o:["Move the critical details to the start of the prompt where they hold attention","Ask the model to read the whole of the prompt carefully before answering","Shorten every other part of the prompt so that the details stand out more","Repeat the case details several times throughout the whole body of the prompt"],c:[0],e:"Content buried mid-prompt is the weakest position. Relocating the material that matters to the front is the direct remedy for the lost-in-the-middle effect.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Which tool_choice setting forces the agent to call some tool but lets it pick which?",o:["any","none","a specific named tool","auto"],c:[0],e:"'any' requires a tool call while leaving the selection to the model; 'auto' permits a text-only reply, and naming a tool removes the choice entirely.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The team wants to add a second agent that reviews the first agent's proposed refunds. What makes this effective?",o:["The reviewer uses a faster model tier so the added step stays cheap","The reviewer runs with its own context and a distinct reviewing brief","The reviewer is the same agent asked to re-examine its own decision","The reviewer sees only the refund amount without the surrounding case"],c:[1],e:"Independent review needs separate context and a genuinely different brief. Self-review inherits the reasoning that produced the decision in the first place.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of letting the agent's conversation history grow without management?",o:["The conversation becomes unrecoverable if the session is interrupted","Tool definitions are dropped once the history passes a certain size","The agent begins repeating its earlier responses back to the customer","Early instructions lose influence as later content accumulates"],c:[3],e:"Unmanaged growth dilutes the instructions that were set at the outset, which is why long-running agents need active context management.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The refund tool and a separate 'goodwill credit' tool have nearly identical descriptions. What follows?",o:["The agent will always choose whichever tool is defined first","The API will reject the request for containing ambiguous tools","The agent will route between them unpredictably","The agent will call both tools and let the application decide"],c:[2],e:"Near-identical descriptions leave the model without a basis for choosing, which shows up as unpredictable routing rather than a defined fallback or an error.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two belong in the agent loop rather than in the prompt? (Select 2)",o:["Checking the stop reason to decide whether to continue","Explaining what the company's returns window is","Describing the tone the agent should use with customers","Enforcing that eligibility is verified before a refund runs"],c:[0,3],e:"Control flow and hard business constraints belong in code; tone and policy content are prompt and knowledge concerns.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A case is escalated to a human. What should be passed along with it?",o:["The agent's confidence score for the answer it could not give","What was attempted, what was established, and why the case was escalated","The full untrimmed transcript of the entire agent conversation","A one-line summary of the customer's original opening message to the agent"],c:[1],e:"A structured handover of attempts, findings, and the escalation reason is what makes the human's job possible without re-reading everything.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The order lookup tool currently returns a paragraph of prose. What is the main argument for returning defined fields instead?",o:["Prose cannot be returned by a tool under the Messages API","Defined fields are routed through a faster processing path","Prose results are discarded from the conversation after one turn","A downstream check can verify a field, but not a sentence"],c:[3],e:"Machine-checkable fields are what let the refund path validate a value before acting on it; prose can only be re-interpreted.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team building the support agent wants Claude Code restricted to reading and testing, never editing, during investigation work. What enforces that?",o:["A review of the diff before any of the changes are committed","An allowed-tools configuration listing only the read and test tools","A reminder in each prompt that this session is investigation only","A line in the project instructions asking it not to edit files"],c:[1],e:"Tool permissions are enforced by configuration; instructions and after-the-fact review do not prevent an edit from being made.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants to resume yesterday's Claude Code session on the refund tool with its context intact. What supports this?",o:["Re-reading the project configuration at the start of the day","Asking the model to recall what was discussed previously","Pasting yesterday's transcript into a fresh session","Resuming the named session rather than starting a new one"],c:[3],e:"Named session resumption restores the prior context directly. Re-pasting a transcript is lossy and the model cannot recall a session it no longer holds.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A tool used by the support agent returns amounts in cents, but the team wants them normalised to pounds before the model sees them. Where does that belong?",o:["In the system prompt, instructing the model to divide by one hundred","In the model's own reasoning, which can handle the conversion inline","In a post-tool hook that transforms the result before it reaches the model","In the tool's description, explaining that the amounts arrive in cents"],c:[2],e:"Deterministic normalisation belongs in a hook where it always happens, rather than depending on the model applying a conversion correctly every time.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team wants the agent's policy document pulled into its Claude Code context without pasting it each time. What mechanism fits?",o:["An instruction telling the model to open the file when relevant","An import reference in the project configuration pointing at the file","A slash command that prints the whole document into the session","A copy of the document committed into the project's root directory"],c:[1],e:"An import pulls the file's content into context as configuration is loaded, rather than depending on a manual step or the model choosing to open it.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The agent's replies are accurate but frequently miss the customer's actual question. What should be measured to confirm this?",o:["Whether the reply is shorter than the average reply length","Whether the reply cites the policy document it drew on","Whether the reply addresses the request the ticket actually made","Whether the reply is factually correct on the points it does make"],c:[2],e:"Relevance to the question asked is a separate property from factual accuracy, and it is the one failing here.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"An extraction of the customer's order number fails schema validation. What should the agent do before giving up?",o:["Retry once with the validation error fed back as part of the request","Escalate immediately, since a validation failure indicates a real fault","Return the malformed value and let the downstream step handle it","Retry the identical request, since the failure may have been transient"],c:[0],e:"Feeding the validation error back gives the model the information it needs to correct itself, which an identical retry does not.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A sample of agent conversations is being reviewed for quality. How should the sample be selected?",o:["The most recent conversations, since they reflect current behaviour","Stratified across case types, so each is represented in the review","Conversations the agent itself flagged as difficult to handle","The longest conversations, since they contain the most to assess"],c:[1],e:"Stratified sampling ensures rare but important case types are examined; recency, length, and self-flagging all skew towards a subset.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A tool call returns an error flagged as retryable. What should the loop do?",o:["Ignore the flag, since the agent cannot act on error categories","Treat it the same as any other error and stop the conversation","Retry with backoff, then surface the failure if it persists","Retry immediately and indefinitely until the call succeeds"],c:[2],e:"A retryable classification is actionable: retry with backoff, but bounded, so a persistent failure still surfaces rather than looping forever.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"m",q:"Which two belong in the case facts block that is never summarised? (Select 2)",o:["The customer's tone across the conversation so far","A summary of what the agent has already tried","The order number and the amount in dispute","The policy clause the case is being decided under"],c:[2,3],e:"Exact transactional values and the governing rule must survive verbatim; tone and attempt history are exactly what summarisation may safely compress.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Team-wide coding standards keep failing to reach new joiners. Where should they live?",o:["In each developer's user-level configuration on their own machine","In a wiki page that new joiners are asked to read during onboarding","In project-level configuration committed to the repository","In the prompt each developer writes at the start of a session"],c:[2],e:"User-level config stays on one machine and never reaches anyone else. Project-level configuration travels with the repository, which is what makes standards shared.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Test conventions need to apply across many directories that don't share a parent. What fits best?",o:["A single root-level file listing every directory the rules apply to","A directory-level configuration file placed in each affected folder","A user-level file maintained by whoever owns the test suite","Path-specific rules matched by glob patterns across the codebase"],c:[3],e:"Glob-matched rules apply across a codebase regardless of location, whereas directory-level files are bound to where they sit.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer is starting a multi-file migration with an unclear approach. What should they use?",o:["Plan mode, to explore the approach before any changes are committed","A separate session for each file so the changes stay independent","Direct execution, since the migration will need many edits anyway","A single very detailed prompt describing every edit in advance"],c:[0],e:"Plan mode suits work where the right approach needs establishing first; direct execution fits clear, contained tasks.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What belongs in project configuration rather than in a Skill?",o:["Instructions for a workflow used by only one team member","Standards that apply to all work in the repository","The procedure for a specific recurring task like a release","A step-by-step guide for generating a particular report"],c:[1],e:"Universal standards belong in configuration; specific repeatable procedures are what Skills package.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A code review prompt says 'be conservative about flagging issues' and results are inconsistent. What is the fix?",o:["Instruct it to flag fewer issues than it did on the previous run","Move to a more capable model tier to improve its judgement","Replace it with categorical criteria stating exactly what to flag","Ask for a confidence score attached to each issue that is raised"],c:[2],e:"Vague confidence language isn't actionable. Explicit categorical criteria — what counts as an issue — is what produces consistency.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated documentation is inconsistent in structure despite detailed written instructions. What usually helps more?",o:["A longer and more precise set of written instructions","A request to re-read the instructions before generating","Two or three concrete examples showing the intended output","A rule forbidding any deviation from the described structure"],c:[2],e:"Concrete examples anchor format more reliably than adding further prose instruction, which is a well-established pattern in prompt design.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A Skill produces very verbose intermediate output that clutters the main session. What addresses this?",o:["Running the Skill in a separate terminal session entirely","Asking the Skill to produce less intermediate output","Truncating the Skill's output before it reaches the session","Running the Skill in an isolated sub-agent context"],c:[3],e:"Context isolation keeps a verbose Skill's working output out of the main conversation while still returning its result.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An engineer's session has drifted and Claude is applying conventions from an unrelated part of the repo. What is the cause?",o:["The model has been switched mid-session without the engineer noticing","Accumulated context from earlier work is still influencing the session","The repository's configuration files conflict with one another","The engineer's user-level configuration has overridden the project's"],c:[1],e:"Carried-over context from earlier tasks is the usual cause of convention bleed, which is why a fresh session helps.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Which tool should be used to find files whose contents match a pattern?",o:["A filename matching tool such as glob","A general-purpose file reading tool","A content search tool such as grep","A directory listing of the repository root"],c:[2],e:"Content search and filename matching are distinct; using the filename tool to search contents is a common and avoidable mistake.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Two configuration files apply to the same file being edited. How should this be designed?",o:["With the two merged into a single file to avoid the overlap entirely","With the more specific configuration layering on top of the broader one","With whichever configuration was modified most recently taking effect","With only the most specific configuration applying and the other ignored"],c:[1],e:"Cascading configuration is the intended model: broader guidance applies, with more specific guidance refining it closer to the code.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Every code generation run sends the same large standards document ahead of a small changing task. How should the prompt be arranged?",o:["Put the standards first, so the fixed prefix is cached across runs","Put the task first, so the model reads the immediate request soonest of all","Interleave the standards with the task so each rule sits beside its use","Trim the standards to whichever sections the current task appears to need"],c:[0],e:"Caching matches on an exact prefix, so stable material has to come first and the variable part last. Reordering per request, or trimming the block to fit the task, changes the prefix and forfeits the reuse.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long refactoring session is losing track of decisions made an hour earlier. What is the practical remedy?",o:["Continue and re-explain each decision whenever it becomes relevant","Break the refactor into files handled in completely separate sessions","Ask the model to recall the decisions it made earlier in the session","Capture the decisions in a persistent form the session can re-read"],c:[3],e:"Persisting decisions where they can be re-read keeps them authoritative, rather than relying on them surviving in a long conversation.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The team wants Claude Code to reach an internal service. What is the appropriate mechanism?",o:["Configuration listing the service's endpoints for reference","An MCP server exposing that service's operations as tools","A prompt describing the service's API in sufficient detail","A Skill containing instructions for calling the service by hand"],c:[1],e:"MCP is the standard integration path for reaching an external system; Skills and prompts describe procedure rather than providing access.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team judge whether a change to their shared configuration helped?",o:["By counting how many review comments were raised the following week","By running a fixed set of representative tasks before and after","By checking whether the configuration file is shorter than before","By asking engineers whether the change felt like an improvement"],c:[1],e:"A controlled before-and-after on representative tasks is what makes the comparison meaningful; perception and incidental counts are confounded.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A release procedure used only by the release manager is added to project-wide configuration. What is the cost?",o:["Project configuration stops being read once a Skill exists for the task","A procedure of that kind cannot be expressed in configuration at all","The configuration file exceeds the maximum length that is permitted","Every session carries it, including the many that will never run a release"],c:[3],e:"Project configuration loads for all work in the repository, so a narrow procedure placed there occupies context in every unrelated session.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants Claude Code to run a multi-step task without confirming each edit. What should they consider?",o:["Whether the task will complete any faster than it would do interactively","Whether the repository has been fully backed up before the run is started","Whether the permission bounds configured still cover the risk of those edits","Whether the particular model tier that was selected supports autonomous operation"],c:[2],e:"Reducing per-step confirmation still operates inside configured permissions, so the question is whether those bounds are appropriate for the work.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should large generated diffs be reviewed in smaller pieces?",o:["Large diffs cannot be displayed correctly by most review tools","The model produces lower-quality code later in a long generation","Version control systems reject any diff above a certain size threshold","Reviewer attention degrades over a long diff, so errors slip through"],c:[3],e:"The constraint is human review capacity. Breaking work into reviewable units is what keeps the check meaningful.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two make a tool set easier for Claude Code to use correctly? (Select 2)",o:["Tool names follow the internal service naming convention","Every available tool is exposed so nothing is ever unavailable","Each tool's description states explicitly when not to use it","The set is scoped to the tools the current task actually needs"],c:[2,3],e:"Explicit boundaries and a scoped set both improve selection accuracy; exposing everything degrades it, and naming conventions are neutral.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A generated test suite passes but does not actually exercise the edge cases. What does this indicate?",o:["The model tier being used is insufficient for test generation work","Generated tests are inherently unable to cover edge cases","The request specified tests but not what they needed to cover","The existing code is untestable in its current structure"],c:[2],e:"Coverage is a requirement that has to be stated. Asking for tests without specifying what they must exercise leaves the selection to the model.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Where should a developer's personal preferences, such as their preferred commit message style, be configured?",o:["At project level, so that the entire team benefits from the convention","In a Skill, so that it can be invoked whenever any commit is being made","In the repository README file, where any other developer is able to read it","At user level, where it applies to their own work without affecting others"],c:[3],e:"Personal preference belongs at user level precisely because it should not be imposed on the team through shared configuration.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An autonomous coding loop keeps editing, re-running tests, and editing again without converging. What is the appropriate control?",o:["A more capable model tier, which converges on a fix more reliably","A stronger instruction telling the model to stop once tests pass","An iteration bound that halts the loop and surfaces the state for a person","A longer overall timeout, so the loop has room to reach a passing state"],c:[2],e:"A non-converging loop needs a hard stop that hands control back. Instructions do not bound it, and more capability or time lets it churn for longer.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants Claude Code to stop and ask before running any command that touches the network. What achieves this?",o:["A review of the session transcript once the work is finished","A note in the project configuration describing the team's policy","A permission rule requiring confirmation for that class of command","A prompt instruction asking it to check before going online"],c:[2],e:"Confirmation gates are permission configuration; prompt-level requests and after-the-fact review do not stop the command running.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A coding agent delegates test-writing to a subagent. What should the subagent receive?",o:["Only the file path, leaving the subagent to discover what the code actually does","The code under test and the coverage it must achieve, not the parent's full history","The parent's entire session, so it has all the surrounding context","The parent's tool set in full, so it is never blocked mid-task"],c:[1],e:"A subagent needs its own brief and success criterion. Passing the whole parent session reintroduces the noise isolation is meant to remove, and passing too little forces rediscovery.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An agentic coding run should stop once the test suite passes. What should signal completion?",o:["A fixed number of iterations after which the loop halts","The test command's exit status, checked by the loop","The model stating in its response that the work is finished","The absence of further edits in the most recent turn"],c:[1],e:"A deterministic external signal is what should end the loop; the model's own account of being finished is not a reliable stopping condition.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An MCP tool returns a result marked as an error rather than throwing. What should the agent loop do with it?",o:["Read the error flag and decide, rather than treating it as a valid result","Treat every returned payload as a successful result to reason over","Terminate the run, since any tool error indicates a broken integration","Retry the call automatically until some non-error result comes back"],c:[0],e:"An error-flagged result is still a result; the loop has to inspect the flag rather than pass the payload on as though the call succeeded.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long coding session keeps re-reading the same large file. What reduces the waste?",o:["Summarising the whole file and discarding the original content","Splitting the file into smaller ones so each read is cheaper","Re-reading it each turn so the content is guaranteed to be current","Reading the relevant section once and keeping it in working context"],c:[3],e:"Repeated full reads of unchanged content are pure overhead; retaining the relevant part avoids paying for it every turn.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"m",q:"Which two belong in a Skill rather than in configuration? (Select 2)",o:["The coding standards every change in the repository must meet","A worked example of the team's preferred migration write-up","The steps for cutting a release, used a few times a month","The list of MCP servers the project connects to"],c:[1,2],e:"Skills package procedures and examples that load only when the task arises; standards and server connections apply to all work and belong in configuration.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A subagent is given a research subtask but produces work unrelated to the wider question. What is the likely cause?",o:["The subagent's tools were too narrowly scoped for the subtask","The subagent used a different model tier from the coordinator","The subagent inherited the coordinator's context and became confused","The coordinator did not pass enough context in the subagent's brief"],c:[3],e:"Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's conversation, so everything they need must be passed explicitly in their prompt.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A report omits a topic area the question required. The coordinator's delegation log shows no subtask was ever created for it. What does this establish?",o:["The fault is in the subagents, which failed to cover their material","The fault is in synthesis, which dropped the section when combining","The fault is in decomposition, since the work was never assigned","The fault is in the sources, which did not contain that topic"],c:[2],e:"The delegation log is the evidence that distinguishes the causes: with no subtask created, nothing downstream could have covered it.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"How should the coordinator launch three independent research subagents efficiently?",o:["Launch them one at a time, waiting for each to return","Emit the three delegation calls together in one response","Launch the first and let it spawn the other two in turn","Combine the three subtasks into one larger subagent brief"],c:[1],e:"Issuing multiple delegation calls in a single response runs them in parallel and avoids serial round-trip latency.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Can two subagents exchange findings directly with one another?",o:["Yes, provided they were launched within the same parallel batch","No — communication routes through the coordinator that dispatched them","No, unless the coordinator has finished and released control of the run","Yes, if they are configured to share a common context window"],c:[1],e:"Hub-and-spoke orchestration routes everything through the coordinator; subagents have no direct channel between them.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A subagent's exploration produced many dead ends. What should be returned to the coordinator?",o:["The complete working context so the coordinator can audit it","Only a numeric confidence score for the subtask's conclusion","The findings and their sources, not the full exploration trail","The raw text of every source the subagent opened while working"],c:[2],e:"Returning the distilled result is the point of isolation; passing back the whole trail reintroduces the noise it was meant to contain.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The report contains a claim no one can trace to a source. What design change prevents this?",o:["Instruct the model not to include unsourced claims in the report","Require subagents to return each finding with its source attached","Ask the coordinator to verify every claim during synthesis","Run the synthesis step twice and keep only the claims in both"],c:[1],e:"Attribution has to be carried from where the finding was made. Asking synthesis to verify after the fact cannot recover a source that was never captured.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"How many tools should each research subagent be given?",o:["Exactly one, so its behaviour stays entirely predictable","As many as the coordinator itself has been given","All available tools, so it is never blocked mid-task","A small set relevant to its role, typically a handful"],c:[3],e:"Scoping each subagent to the few tools its role needs preserves selection reliability; a large set degrades it.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A synthesis agent has been given web search tools. What is the concern?",o:["Search results cannot be combined with the findings returned by subagents","Its role is to integrate findings, so search widens its remit unnecessarily","A synthesis agent is not permitted to call any tools of any kind at all","Two separate agents searching over the same sources duplicates the work"],c:[1],e:"Tools should match role. Giving the synthesiser search capability blurs responsibility and invites it to redo the researchers' work.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A subagent fails partway through its subtask. What should the coordinator receive?",o:["Nothing, so the coordinator retries the subtask from the beginning","A generic error so the coordinator can decide how to proceed","The failure type, what was attempted, and any partial findings","The full stack trace from the underlying tool that failed"],c:[2],e:"Rich failure propagation lets the coordinator decide intelligently; silence forces blind retries and a bare error discards recoverable work.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"When is a multi-agent design the wrong choice for a research question?",o:["When the answer needs to be traceable back to its evidence","When the research is expected to take longer than a few minutes","When the relevant sources are spread across many different systems","When the question is narrow enough for one agent to answer directly"],c:[3],e:"Orchestration overhead is only justified by genuinely separable work; a narrow question doesn't benefit from being split.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the quality of the research system be evaluated?",o:["By the number of distinct sources each run manages to consult","Against questions with known good answers, scored on coverage and accuracy","By whether the finished report reads as fluent and well organised throughout","By how long a complete research run takes from start to finish"],c:[1],e:"Known-answer questions scored on substantive criteria measure quality; source counts, runtime, and fluency are proxies that can all improve while accuracy falls.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Findings from six subagents exceed what fits comfortably in the synthesis prompt. What is the sound approach?",o:["Drop the findings from whichever subagents returned the least","Have subagents return structured summaries sized for synthesis","Synthesise in pairs, combining two subagents' findings at a time","Move to a model with a larger context window and pass everything"],c:[1],e:"Designing the return format to fit the synthesis step is the structural fix; simply enlarging the window defers the same problem.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A subagent's document-store tool returns results without indicating which are most relevant. What should the tool return instead?",o:["Only the single best match, so no judgement is needed at all","Ranked results carrying the score or ordering the store applied","Every matching document, leaving the subagent to judge relevance","The store's query syntax, so the subagent can refine the search"],c:[1],e:"Passing the store's own ranking through preserves information the subagent cannot reconstruct, without forcing it to read everything.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What determines when the coordinator's own loop should stop?",o:["Whether a fixed maximum number of subagents has been reached","Whether the finished report has reached a target length measured in words","Whether the elapsed time has passed a configured threshold","Whether every delegated subtask has returned and synthesis is complete"],c:[3],e:"Completion is defined by the work being done, with caps and timeouts acting as safety nets rather than the primary condition.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The system produces good reports on technical questions and poor ones on policy questions. What should be investigated?",o:["Whether the report template needs a different structure for policy","Whether the model tier is adequate for policy subject matter","Whether decomposition and source selection suit that question type","Whether policy questions are inherently unsuited to the system"],c:[2],e:"A systematic difference by question type points at how those questions are broken down and which sources are consulted.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"m",q:"A research agent fetches pages from the open web, and some contain text addressed to the agent telling it to abandon its brief. Which three controls address this? (Select 3)",o:["Treat retrieved text as untrusted data rather than as instructions to follow","Keep the agent's own instructions in the trusted prompt, never in fetched content","Limit what the agent can do after reading, so an injected instruction has no reach","Rely on the model noticing the manipulation and declining to act on it"],c:[0,1,2],e:"Retrieved content is data. Labelling it as such, keeping instructions in the trusted prompt, and bounding what the agent can do afterwards all hold however convincing the injected text is. Model judgement alone is not a control.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Two subagents return contradictory findings on the same point. What should the coordinator do?",o:["Re-run both subagents until the two findings come into agreement","Discard both of the findings and omit the point from the final report","Accept whichever finding came from the subagent that ran longer","Surface the disagreement and the evidence rather than silently choosing"],c:[3],e:"A genuine evidential conflict is information. Concealing it by arbitrary selection or omission misrepresents the state of the research.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"What is the most useful thing to log from a research run?",o:["The wall-clock duration of each individual subagent's work","The total number of tokens that were consumed across the whole of the run","Which subtasks were created, what each returned, and from which sources","The final report only, since intermediate steps are transient"],c:[2],e:"Logging the decomposition, returns, and sources is what makes a run diagnosable when the report turns out to be wrong.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The coordinator delegates a subtask that is itself highly complex. What is a reasonable design response?",o:["Handle it within the coordinator directly rather than delegating it out","Increase the subagent's tool allocation in order to compensate for it","Split it arbitrarily into equal parts spread across several subagents","Let that subagent decompose further rather than forcing a flat structure"],c:[3],e:"Nested decomposition matches structure to the problem; arbitrary splitting cuts across natural boundaries and extra tools don't address complexity.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The coordinator's context fills with raw subagent transcripts before synthesis begins. What is the direct consequence?",o:["Prompt caching is invalidated on every turn of the synthesis step it runs","The coordinator is barred from reading any subagent's working notes","The coordinator becomes unable to interpret the subagents' findings","The findings it must weigh compete with working material already distilled"],c:[3],e:"Synthesis needs the findings in view; undistilled transcripts occupy the same budget without adding anything the summaries lack.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants a repeatable command that kicks off a standard research run with preset arguments. What fits?",o:["A project instruction describing the steps a research run follows","A custom slash command carrying an argument hint for its parameters","A shell alias that launches Claude Code with a long prompt string","A saved text file the engineer pastes at the start of each session"],c:[1],e:"Slash commands package a reusable invocation, and an argument hint tells the user what it expects. The alternatives rely on manual steps each time.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A long research session is approaching its context limit but the work is not finished. Which action preserves progress?",o:["Compacting the session so earlier turns are condensed rather than lost","Deleting the earliest messages manually until the session fits again","Starting a fresh session and re-describing the work completed so far","Reducing the number of subagents so less context is generated"],c:[0],e:"Compaction condenses the history while keeping what matters. Restarting discards established findings and manual deletion is indiscriminate.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A subagent's exploration should run without polluting the main research session. Which configuration achieves this?",o:["Limiting the subagent to a smaller number of available tools","Running the subagent in a second terminal window alongside the first","Defining the agent so its work runs in a forked, isolated context","Instructing the subagent to summarise before returning anything"],c:[2],e:"A forked context keeps the subagent's working turns out of the parent session entirely, which instructions and tool limits do not achieve.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A long research session is interrupted and the analyst returns to it the following day. What lets the work continue rather than restart?",o:["Resuming the saved session, which restores the earlier conversation state","Starting fresh and pasting the previous session's closing summary back in","Re-running the original opening prompt and hoping the same path is taken","Leaving the session window open indefinitely so that nothing is ever lost"],c:[0],e:"Sessions persist and can be resumed with their state intact. A pasted summary loses the intermediate reasoning, re-running the prompt relies on the path repeating, and holding a window open is not a recovery mechanism.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A search tool intermittently returns an error the coordinator cannot interpret. What should the tool return instead?",o:["A structured error identifying the category and whether a retry may help","A plain-text message describing in prose what happened to go wrong","The underlying exception text taken from the service that failed","An empty result, so that the coordinator simply proceeds on without it"],c:[0],e:"A structured error category lets the loop decide between retrying, substituting, or surfacing; prose and raw exceptions leave that judgement to guesswork.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A research subagent calls an MCP tool that returns a large result set on every query. What reduces the context cost without losing recall?",o:["Call the tool less often and rely on the model's own knowledge between calls","Have the server return a page of results with a handle for fetching more","Have the server return only the single highest-scoring result each time","Truncate the result text in the client before the model reads it"],c:[1],e:"Paging keeps the context small while leaving the rest reachable. Returning one result or truncating mid-text both discard information the subagent may need.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The research report's quality varies with how the opening question is phrased. What should be standardised?",o:["The maximum length the finished report is allowed to reach","A prompt template capturing the framing that reliably works","The model tier used for the coordinator on every research run","The number of subagents the coordinator is permitted to launch"],c:[1],e:"If phrasing drives quality, capturing the effective phrasing in a reusable template is what makes results consistent.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two make a coordinator's delegation worth its overhead? (Select 2)",o:["Each subtask needs sources or expertise the others do not","The coordinator has more tools available than any one subagent","The overall question is long enough to fill a substantial report","The subtasks can be worked on without waiting for one another"],c:[0,3],e:"Independence and genuinely distinct expertise are what delegation buys. Report length and the coordinator's tool count say nothing about whether the work divides.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Different teams need access to different internal systems. How should this be handled?",o:["Scope each team's tool access to the systems their role requires","Expose every MCP server and rely on teams to use the right one","Grant full access and audit usage after the fact each month","Build one merged server exposing all systems behind a single tool"],c:[0],e:"Least-privilege scoping is both a security control and a reliability one, since a smaller relevant tool set improves selection accuracy.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A team proposes building a custom MCP server for a widely used internal wiki. What most affects whether that is the right call?",o:["Which of the two available MCP transports the new server would be built on","Whether an existing server already covers it, and who would maintain a new one","How many engineers are expected to query the wiki in a typical week","Whether the wiki's total content would fit inside a context window"],c:[1],e:"Build-versus-adopt turns on prior art and the ongoing maintenance burden; transport and volume are downstream implementation details.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks a question that spans three connected systems. What makes this work reliably?",o:["The engineer states which system should be queried in their question","The agent queries all three of the systems and combines whatever returns","All three systems are merged behind one tool with a mode parameter","Each system's tools are clearly described so the model routes correctly"],c:[3],e:"Clear, distinct descriptions are what let the model route across systems without the user having to direct it manually.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool explores a large unfamiliar codebase and runs out of context before finishing. What is the design fix?",o:["Restrict the tool to repositories below a fixed size","Load the entire codebase at the start of every session","Switch to the model with the largest available context window","Explore progressively, loading only what each step requires"],c:[3],e:"Progressive discovery keeps context proportional to the question, whereas monolithic loading exhausts it regardless of window size.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Answers about a legacy service are confidently wrong because its documentation is outdated. What addresses this?",o:["Instruct the model to treat all documentation as unreliable","Remove the documentation so only the code is ever consulted","Ask engineers to update the documentation before using the tool","Ground answers in the code itself and flag where docs disagree"],c:[3],e:"Preferring authoritative sources while surfacing disagreement gives engineers the real picture, rather than hiding the conflict.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A tool that queries a production database is being added. What control matters most?",o:["Logging every query it runs for review by the team afterwards","Restricting it to read operations that cannot alter production data","Ensuring its description warns the model to be careful with it","Limiting the number of times it may be called within a single session"],c:[1],e:"A technical restriction to read-only is enforceable; warnings, logging, and call limits don't prevent a destructive statement executing.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool should answer from internal systems only, but sometimes answers from general knowledge. What is the fix?",o:["Ask engineers to phrase questions so that only internal data applies","Instruct it to answer only from retrieved results and say when it cannot","Reduce the model tier so that it relies rather less on its own knowledge","Connect more internal systems so that more questions can be answered"],c:[1],e:"Bounding the model to retrieved content with an explicit fallback is the direct control on ungrounded answers.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should retrieved results include where they came from?",o:["Sources are required before results can be cached for reuse","Engineers can verify the answer against the system it came from","Sources reduce the number of tokens that each result consumes","Retrieval fails silently unless each result carries a source"],c:[1],e:"Traceability is what makes an answer checkable, which matters most where the tool is being trusted about unfamiliar systems.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team measure whether the tool is genuinely useful?",o:["Whether engineers reach correct answers faster than without it","How many queries the tool handles across the organisation","How many internal systems the tool has been connected to in total","How positively engineers rate the tool in a survey"],c:[0],e:"Time to a correct answer is the outcome the tool exists to improve; usage, coverage, and sentiment can all rise without it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Two connected MCP servers each expose a tool named 'search', and the agent has started calling the wrong one. What resolves this?",o:["Namespacing each tool so its server of origin is part of the name","Removing one of the two servers until the agent stops confusing them","Instructing the agent in the prompt which 'search' it should prefer","Ordering the servers so the intended one is always offered first"],c:[0],e:"Two identically named tools give the model nothing to choose between. Qualifying the names restores the distinction, where prompt rules and ordering leave the ambiguity in place.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long exploration session has accumulated many file contents no longer relevant. What helps?",o:["Summarising all of the file contents down into a single block","Clearing superseded results from context as the task moves on","Asking the model to ignore the files it no longer needs","Starting a new session whenever the context becomes large"],c:[1],e:"Actively removing superseded content is more precise than summarising everything or discarding the whole session.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Different teams need different architectural conventions applied to their own repositories. How should this be structured?",o:["Each repository carries its own configuration alongside the code it governs","Each engineer configures the conventions for the teams they work with","One central configuration file lists the conventions for every team","The MCP server's tool descriptions each carry that team's own conventions"],c:[0],e:"Configuration living with the code keeps conventions correct as repositories move between teams, which a central list or per-engineer setup cannot.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks the read-only tooling to apply a fix rather than explain one. What is the appropriate behaviour?",o:["Escalate the request to a senior engineer for their approval","Refuse the request without explaining what the change would be","Explain the change and stop, since write capability was never granted","Apply the change and then report on what was modified afterwards"],c:[2],e:"The tool should do everything within its granted scope — which includes describing the fix — and simply not act beyond it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Answers about one particular system are consistently poor. What should be examined?",o:["Whether that system is inherently too complex to answer about","Whether retrieval over that system is returning the right material","Whether the model tier needs raising for that system only","Whether engineers happen to ask worse questions about that system"],c:[1],e:"A system-specific failure points at that system's integration and retrieval quality rather than at the model or the users.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two make an MCP server easier to keep working as the systems behind it change? (Select 2)",o:["Returning the underlying system's raw response without reshaping it","A stable tool contract that hides how the underlying system is called","Versioning the server so a breaking change does not surprise its clients","Adding a tool for each new endpoint as the upstream system grows"],c:[1,2],e:"A stable contract and explicit versioning absorb upstream change; passing raw responses through and adding a tool per endpoint push every change onto every client.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks a question whose answer depends on which environment they mean. What should the tool do?",o:["Answer for every environment and let the engineer select","Ask which environment before answering","Answer generally without reference to any specific environment","Answer for production, since that is the most commonly meant"],c:[1],e:"A material ambiguity should be resolved rather than guessed, since a confidently wrong environment-specific answer is worse than a question.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of the tool caching answers about internal systems?",o:["Cached answers cannot be attributed to a specific engineer","Systems change, so a cached answer can become quietly wrong","Cached answers consume more context than fresh retrieval","Caching prevents the tool from citing its sources correctly"],c:[1],e:"Staleness is the real hazard when caching answers about systems that evolve independently of the cache.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team decide which internal system to integrate next?",o:["By which systems are easiest to build a connector for","By which systems have the best existing documentation","By which team has asked most recently for an integration","By which systems engineers most often need answers about"],c:[3],e:"Demand for answers is the value signal; ease of integration and documentation quality affect cost, not benefit.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An autonomous investigation occasionally runs for hours and makes far more tool calls than anticipated. What should bound it?",o:["Explicit ceilings on tool calls, time and cost, enforced as it runs","Prompt wording asking the agent to work efficiently and stop when finished","A smaller model, so that each individual step costs less than it does now","A post-run report showing the team afterwards exactly what the run consumed"],c:[0],e:"A run needs limits it cannot exceed. Asking for efficiency is advisory, a cheaper model changes the unit cost rather than the bound, and a report after the fact arrives too late to stop anything.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A team wants their MCP server reachable by tooling on several machines. Which transport applies?",o:["Streamable HTTP, for a server running as an independent remote process","Either one, since both transports support remote and local use equally","Neither, since an MCP server must run on the machine that calls it","stdio, which runs the server as a local subprocess of the client"],c:[0],e:"stdio is for a local subprocess; reaching a server from several machines is what Streamable HTTP is for.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The tooling needs to declare which MCP servers it connects to, in a form the whole team shares. Where does that belong?",o:["Each engineer's personal settings on their own workstation","An environment variable set on the deployment host only","An MCP configuration file committed with the project","A section of the project's README describing the servers"],c:[2],e:"A committed MCP configuration file is what makes server connections consistent and reviewable across the team.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An MCP server needs an API token that must not be committed to the repository. How should the configuration reference it?",o:["Through an environment variable the configuration expands at load time","As a placeholder value each engineer replaces before running","In a separate file added to the repository's ignore list","As an encrypted string, with the decryption key stored alongside it"],c:[0],e:"Environment-variable expansion keeps the secret out of the file entirely, whereas the alternatives either rely on manual steps or still ship the secret.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Engineers want the tooling to expose a reference document that Claude can read on demand rather than loading it every session. What fits?",o:["A tool that returns the document's full contents on every call","A Skill containing the document's text in its instructions","An MCP resource the server exposes for retrieval when needed","An import that pulls the document into context at session start"],c:[2],e:"Resources are MCP's read-on-demand data category, which is exactly the case for reference material that should not occupy context by default.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tooling should answer questions but never modify the systems it reads from. How is that guaranteed?",o:["By instructing the agent in its prompt that it must not change anything","By logging every action so unwanted changes can be reversed","By having an engineer confirm each action before it proceeds","By granting only read-capable tools, with no write operations exposed"],c:[3],e:"Capability is bounded by which tools exist. Instructions, logs, and confirmations do not remove the ability to write.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The tooling gives fluent answers that engineers cannot trace to any internal system. What should be measured?",o:["The share of answers whose claims are supported by a retrieved source","How quickly the tooling returns an answer to the engineer","The average length of the answers the tooling produces","The number of separate internal systems that each answer draws upon"],c:[0],e:"Groundedness is the property in question: what proportion of claims trace to retrieved material. Length, breadth, and latency say nothing about it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"m",q:"Which two signals suggest an exploration session should be restarted rather than continued? (Select 2)",o:["Earlier conclusions in the session are now known to be wrong","The topic has moved to a system unrelated to the earlier work","The engineer has asked more than a dozen questions in it","The session has been running for more than an hour"],c:[0,1],e:"A change of subject or a poisoned premise makes the accumulated context actively misleading. Elapsed time and question count alone say nothing about whether it still helps.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The pipeline job hangs indefinitely when Claude Code runs. What is the most likely cause?",o:["The repository is too large to be analysed within the job timeout","It is running interactively and waiting for input that never arrives","The pipeline lacks the permission needed to read the repository contents","The model tier selected is not available to automated pipelines"],c:[1],e:"Non-interactive execution has to be requested explicitly; without it the process waits for input a pipeline can never supply.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Downstream pipeline steps need to parse the review output. What should be requested?",o:["Plain prose, which downstream steps can search with pattern matching","A defined structured output format the next step can consume","Whatever format the model considers clearest for a human reader","Output written directly into the pull request as review comments"],c:[1],e:"Machine-consumed output needs a defined structure; parsing prose with pattern matching is fragile and breaks on ordinary variation.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review step should run on every pull request and developers wait on it. Which processing mode applies?",o:["Synchronous calls, since the result is blocking a merge","Batch processing, since it halves the cost of every review","Batch processing, since pull requests arrive in high volume","Whichever mode has the most capacity available at the time"],c:[0],e:"A blocking pre-merge check cannot use asynchronous batch processing; the cost saving is irrelevant if the result arrives too late.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The nightly release-notes job is latency-tolerant and high volume. What suits it?",o:["Batch processing, which trades immediacy for substantially lower cost","Synchronous calls, to keep the job's implementation simple","Streaming, so progress is visible while the job is running","Whichever processing mode the pre-merge review step already makes use of"],c:[0],e:"An overnight, non-blocking workload is exactly the batch case, where the latency tradeoff costs nothing.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The team wants to know whether a change to the review prompt helped, using the pipeline's own history. What makes that comparison sound?",o:["Checking whether the newer version raises fewer trivial findings","Comparing the issue counts from the weeks before and after the change","Re-running both versions over the same past pull requests","Asking engineers whether the review felt more useful afterwards"],c:[2],e:"Replaying both prompts over identical inputs isolates the change. A week-on-week comparison confounds it with whatever code happened to be submitted.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A tool call inside the pipeline fails. What should the job do?",o:["Report the failure clearly and exit in a way the pipeline can act on","Mark the pull request as approved so that merges are not delayed","Continue on silently so that the pipeline is not blocked by the error","Retry indefinitely until the underlying tool becomes available"],c:[0],e:"An unattended job must fail visibly. Silent continuation and auto-approval both let a broken check pass as a working one.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A contributor's personal configuration and the repository's committed configuration give the review step conflicting instructions. Which should the pipeline run under?",o:["The repository's, so every pull request is judged the same way","The contributor's, since they know most about the change being made","Whichever of the two was edited most recently before the run began","Both merged, with any conflict resolved in the contributor's favour"],c:[0],e:"A shared gate only means something if every contributor is measured against the same standard. Personal configuration can shape someone's own session, but it must not travel into a decision about what merges.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team tell whether the automated review is actually catching real problems?",o:["Compare its findings against issues later found in production or review","Count how many issues it raises over the course of a typical week","Ask engineers whether they find the automated review helpful overall","Check that it raises at least one issue on each and every pull request"],c:[0],e:"Validating against problems that genuinely mattered is the real test; volume and sentiment can both look healthy while the check misses what counts.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The triage agent judges whether a build failure is a real regression, and the pipeline blocks merges. Where should the blocking decision sit?",o:["In the pipeline, applying the team's policy to the finding it was handed","In the agent, which holds the most context about the failure it examined","In whichever component happened to detect the failure first in the run","In the agent for clear-cut cases, with the pipeline taking ambiguous ones"],c:[0],e:"The agent classifies; what a classification should cost the team is a separate policy decision belonging to the pipeline. Splitting it across both leaves no single place where the rule is written down.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Review quality varies between pull requests of similar size. What should be examined first?",o:["Whether different engineers on the team write differently structured code","Whether the model is simply inherently inconsistent when reviewing code","Whether the pull requests are submitted at different times of the day","Whether the diff and its surrounding context are being supplied consistently"],c:[3],e:"Inconsistent inputs are the usual driver of inconsistent output, so what actually reaches the model is the first thing to check.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What should the pipeline do when Claude Code's output cannot be parsed?",o:["Retry once with the same input and continue regardless of result","Pass the unparsed output to the next step for it to interpret","Fail the step explicitly rather than proceeding on an assumption","Skip the step and continue the pipeline as though it passed"],c:[2],e:"An unparseable result is a failure and should surface as one; continuing pretends a check ran when it did not.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated release notes occasionally invent a change that was not in the diff. What reduces this?",o:["Adding a disclaimer noting that the release notes are generated automatically","Asking for shorter release notes with fewer individual entries","Grounding the notes strictly in the commit and diff content supplied","Generating the notes twice and keeping entries present in both"],c:[2],e:"Constraining generation to the supplied source is the direct control; length limits and duplicate runs don't prevent invention.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An MCP server should expose build logs the agent reads and a retry action it can invoke. How do these map onto the server's primitives?",o:["The logs as a resource to read, the retry as a tool that acts","Both as tools, since the agent reaches each of them through one server","Both as resources, since each is content the server is making available","The logs as a prompt template, with the retry exposed as a resource to fetch"],c:[0],e:"Resources supply content to read, tools perform actions with effects, and prompts are reusable templates a user invokes. Collapsing the distinction hides which calls change something.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The pipeline logs each run's prompt and output so bad reviews can be diagnosed. What must accompany that to make it safe?",o:["Redaction, access control, and a defined retention period for the logs","An assurance that only build metadata rather than content is retained","A copy of the logs stored in the repository next to the code","A daily summary of the logs circulated to the whole engineering team"],c:[0],e:"Logs of this kind capture proprietary code and internal detail, so diagnostic value has to be paired with controls on who sees them and for how long.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two failure modes should an unattended pipeline step be designed to avoid? (Select 2)",o:["Running without a human reviewer approving each individual run","Producing output in a machine-readable format the next step parses","Remaining blocked indefinitely without surfacing the dependency failure","Passing silently when the check did not actually run"],c:[2,3],e:"A false pass and an unbounded, unexplained block are the two ways an automated gate misleads the team. Whether an unavailable dependency blocks or warns is a deliberate policy choice; failing without ever surfacing why is not.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Review cost has grown with the team. Scoping review to changed files is proposed. What does that approach miss?",o:["Formatting problems that appear only in the unchanged portions","The overall architecture, which no automated review can assess","Consequences in files the diff touches indirectly rather than directly","Issues in files that happened to be changed earlier in the same release"],c:[2],e:"A diff-only pass is cheap but blind to cross-file effects, which is why a focused local pass is usually paired with a broader integration pass.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The review step runs in the pipeline with nobody at the keyboard. What must its configuration settle in advance?",o:["Exactly which tools it may use, since no one can approve a prompt","Which reviewer to notify whenever the step needs a decision to be made","How long it should wait at a permission prompt before the step gives up","Which model to fall back to if the first choice is unavailable"],c:[0],e:"An unattended run cannot ask. Its permitted tools have to be declared up front, or the step either stalls or is handed more access than the job needs. Waiting or notifying reintroduces the human the step exists to work without.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A very large pull request exceeds what fits in one review request. What is the sound approach?",o:["Review only the first portion that fits within the limit","Review it in coherent chunks and aggregate the findings","Reject the pull request and ask for it to be split up","Summarise the diff and review the summary instead"],c:[1],e:"Chunked review with aggregation preserves coverage; truncating loses it, and reviewing a summary reviews the wrong artefact.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What should happen if the review step is unavailable when a pull request is opened?",o:["The pull request simply proceeds, since the step is only advisory anyway","The team decides in advance whether that blocks or merely warns, and encodes it","The pull request stays blocked until the step becomes available once again","The step is retried silently in the background until it eventually succeeds"],c:[1],e:"Whether an unavailable check blocks or warns is a deliberate policy decision that should be made and encoded, not defaulted to.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review gate can be tuned to raise more issues or fewer. What should decide where it is set?",o:["What each error costs this team: a missed defect against a false alarm","The setting that keeps the total number of flagged pull requests lowest","Whichever setting produces the most consistent output between two runs","The rate matching how often the team's human reviewers leave comments"],c:[0],e:"The threshold encodes the relative cost of a missed defect versus wasted review time, and those are rarely equal. Minimising flags, chasing consistency, or copying human volume each optimise something other than that cost.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The triage agent's findings are useful but arrive after the team has already started debugging. What should change?",o:["Give it a more capable model tier so it reaches its conclusion sooner","Run it only on failures that have recurred more than once","Trigger it on the failure event rather than on the next scheduled run","Reduce how much of the build log it reads before forming a conclusion"],c:[2],e:"Value here depends on arriving before the humans do, which is a question of when the agent is triggered rather than how fast or thorough it is.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The pipeline branches on a status field the review step returns, and one run returns a value outside the defined set. What should the pipeline do?",o:["Treat the step as failed and stop, rather than guess what was meant","Map it onto the nearest defined status so that the run can carry on","Default to a pass, since the step did at least produce output of some kind","Re-run the step until it returns a value the branch does recognise"],c:[0],e:"An unrecognised status means the contract broke, and every way of continuing invents a decision nobody made. Stopping surfaces the break, where retrying blindly can loop on a systematic fault.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A build-log MCP tool sometimes returns a partial result when the log is still being written. How should this be represented?",o:["As a result flagged incomplete, so the caller can decide whether to wait","As a normal successful result containing whatever was available","As an empty result, so the step falls back to its default behaviour","As an error, on the basis that an incomplete log cannot support a verdict"],c:[0],e:"Signalling incompleteness lets the caller choose; presenting a partial log as complete invites a verdict based on missing evidence.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A pipeline tool takes a mode parameter selecting one of six unrelated operations. What is the argument for splitting it up?",o:["Each operation can then carry its own description and input schema","Six smaller tools will run faster than one that branches internally","A larger tool set demonstrates that the integration is more capable","Splitting removes the need to validate the inputs each one receives"],c:[0],e:"Selection accuracy comes from a description and schema that describe one thing. A mode switch hides six contracts behind one, leaving the model no guidance about which fits. Speed and validation are unaffected.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Release-note generation is submitted as a large batch. What must accompany each request so results can be matched afterwards?",o:["A sequence number, since results return in submission order","The commit hash alone, which is unique to each release note","A unique identifier supplied with each individual request","A timestamp recorded at the moment each request is submitted"],c:[2],e:"Batch results are not guaranteed to return in order, so a unique per-request identifier is what makes them matchable.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"m",q:"Which two does an engineer need in order to act on a review finding without further investigation? (Select 2)",o:["The reviewer's overall confidence in the finding","A concrete description of the change being asked for","The category the finding has been filed under","The exact location the finding refers to"],c:[1,3],e:"Location and a specific requested change are together sufficient to act. Confidence and category help triage a queue but still leave the engineer to work out what to do.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Extracted invoice data validates cleanly against its schema, yet wrong values still reach the database. What does this show?",o:["Schemas cannot express the field types that an invoice actually requires","Structural validity says nothing about whether a value is correct","Validation applies only to documents above a size threshold","The schema was applied only after the data had been written"],c:[1],e:"A well-formed record with a wrong total passes every structural check, which is why semantic validation has to sit alongside the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A field is marked required in the schema, but some contracts genuinely do not contain it. What risk does this create?",o:["It causes the extraction to fail so that the document is skipped entirely","It causes the schema to be relaxed automatically for that document","It raises the chance the model supplies a value the document does not support","It causes the validator to populate the field with an empty string"],c:[2],e:"A required field with no source value pressures the model towards filling it. Making the field nullable is what lets genuine absence be reported.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Line items extract correctly but the totals do not reconcile. What check catches this?",o:["A stricter schema that requires the total field to be a numeric value","A second extraction run compared against the first for agreement","An arithmetic validation comparing the total against the line items","A confidence score requested from the model for the total field"],c:[2],e:"A semantic consistency check on the relationship between fields is what catches this; type constraints and repeat runs do not.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An extraction tool defines a JSON schema for its input. What does that alone guarantee?",o:["That totals returned will reconcile with the line items they summarise","Nothing on its own — conformance is guaranteed only when strict mode is enabled","That every field will be present and correctly typed on every call","That the values returned are always drawn from the supplied source document"],c:[1],e:"A declared schema describes the expected shape; guaranteed conformance requires strict tool use to be turned on. The semantic guarantees never follow from a schema at all.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Scanned forms of poor quality produce unreliable extractions. What is the sound design response?",o:["Extract anyway and correct the errors when they are noticed downstream","Route low-quality documents to human review rather than extracting blindly","Reject the documents outright and ask the sender to resubmit all of them","Use a more capable model tier on every document in order to compensate"],c:[1],e:"Detecting the condition and routing it differently is what protects the database, since blind extraction pushes the cost downstream.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Should extraction be built as an agent or a fixed workflow?",o:["A workflow, since the steps are known and repeat identically","An agent, so it can decide which fields matter per document","Either, since the two approaches perform identically here","An agent, since documents vary in quality and completeness"],c:[0],e:"Known, repeatable steps favour a workflow. Input variation is handled by validation and routing rather than by dynamic control flow.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the extraction system be evaluated before it goes live?",o:["By checking that every output validates against the schema","By reviewing a sample of the outputs for their general plausibility","Against documents with known correct values across the quality range","By confirming it processes documents faster than manual entry"],c:[2],e:"Known-correct ground truth across representative quality is what measures accuracy; schema validity and plausibility miss semantic errors.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A field is sometimes extracted from the wrong section of a long contract. What helps most?",o:["Instructing where in the document the field is expected to appear","Extracting the field twice and comparing the two results","Requesting a confidence score alongside the extracted value","Shortening the contract before it is passed through for extraction"],c:[0],e:"Locational guidance disambiguates similar-looking content; repeat extraction can make the same mistake twice.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Overnight extraction is submitted as a batch. Which property of that choice most affects the pipeline's design?",o:["Results are not returned by the submitting call, so the pipeline has to poll for them","Results are returned in exactly the order in which the requests were submitted","Failed items are retried automatically until they eventually succeed","Partial results become readable to the caller while the batch is still running"],c:[0],e:"The submission call returns a handle rather than results, so the pipeline polls until processing ends. It may certainly wait — what it cannot do is receive the results synchronously.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the extracted output record which document each value came from?",o:["So that the same document is never processed more than one time","So the model can be prevented from reprocessing old documents","So documents can be deleted once extraction has completed","So a wrong value in the database can be traced back and corrected"],c:[3],e:"Provenance is what makes downstream errors correctable, which matters when incorrect data is expensive to unwind.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A field extracts correctly 97% of the time across a large, representative test set. What can that aggregate figure conceal?",o:["That a more capable model tier would push the figure above 99%","That the errors are expensive to detect once they reach the database","That the test set was too small for the figure to be meaningful","That one document type or source fails far more often than the average implies"],c:[3],e:"An average is a single number over a mixed population, so it hides variation between segments. Sample size is settled by the stem, and cost and model choice are separate decisions rather than things the figure conceals.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Where should a human review step sit in the extraction pipeline?",o:["Before extraction, to check that documents are worth processing","At the end of each day, reviewing everything processed since","After the write, so errors can be corrected in the database","After validation and before anything is written to the database"],c:[3],e:"Review belongs immediately before the irreversible step, which is the point at which correction becomes expensive.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A proposal gives the model a tool that writes extracted records straight to the database. What is the concern?",o:["The model cannot reliably construct valid database statements","A tool is not able to write to an external database under any circumstances","Nothing validates the record between extraction and a costly-to-unwind write","Database writes are too slow to complete inside a tool call"],c:[2],e:"The problem is the missing gate rather than the write itself: validation has to sit between extraction and a write that is expensive to reverse.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Documents arrive in several languages. What should the design account for?",o:["That field labels differ, so location-based rules may not transfer","That extraction accuracy is identical regardless of language","That non-English documents must be translated before extraction","That each language would require a separate model to be selected"],c:[0],e:"Language changes how fields are labelled and laid out, which is what breaks rules tuned to one language's documents.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"m",q:"Which two checks catch errors a schema cannot? (Select 2)",o:["Confirming the output parses as valid JSON","Confirming every field is present and correctly typed","Confirming extracted values actually appear in the source document","Verifying that totals reconcile with their component values"],c:[2,3],e:"Reconciliation and source verification are semantic checks; presence, typing, and parseability are exactly what a schema already covers.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An extraction returns a plausible supplier name that does not appear in the document. What is this?",o:["A fabricated value, which schema validation will not detect","A retrieval failure caused by poor document quality","A formatting error in the way that the field was returned","A schema violation that validation should have rejected"],c:[0],e:"An invented but well-formed value passes validation cleanly, which is why source verification is needed alongside the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Extraction and validation currently run as one combined step. What is the argument for separating them?",o:["Validation cannot be expressed within an extraction prompt","Failures become attributable to extraction or to validation","Separate steps run faster than a single combined one","Combined steps are not supported by the Messages API"],c:[1],e:"Separation makes failure attributable, which is what allows the right component to be fixed.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team handle a document type the system has never seen?",o:["Process it normally, since the schema will catch any problems","Reject it automatically as an unsupported document type","Route it for human handling until it has been evaluated","Process it with a more capable model tier as a precaution"],c:[2],e:"Unvalidated document types shouldn't flow into an expensive-to-unwind database on the assumption that validation will catch everything.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the system record the confidence-relevant conditions of each extraction, such as scan quality?",o:["It allows later errors to be correlated with the conditions that produced them","It reduces the number of documents that end up needing human review","It allows the model to improve its own accuracy on future documents","It is required before any extracted data may be written to the database at all"],c:[0],e:"Recording conditions makes patterns in the failures visible, which is what lets the routing rules be improved.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"What should the extraction tool's schema do about a field that is genuinely optional?",o:["Mark it nullable so absence can be reported honestly","Mark it required and let validation reject documents lacking it","Omit it from the schema and extract it in a separate step","Mark it required and accept an empty string when it is missing"],c:[0],e:"Nullable fields let the model report genuine absence rather than being pushed into inventing a value to satisfy the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The extraction pipeline runs Claude Code unattended and the next step parses its output. Which invocation is required?",o:["Interactive mode, with a longer timeout configured for the job","Non-interactive mode, with the output left as plain prose","Non-interactive mode with a JSON output format specified","Interactive mode, with the output redirected to a file"],c:[2],e:"An unattended run needs non-interactive execution so it never waits for input, and a structured output format so the next step can consume it.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team wants extracted output validated against a fixed shape as it is produced. Which option supports that directly?",o:["Asking the model to confirm its output matches the expected shape","Checking the shape in a later pipeline step and failing if wrong","Supplying a JSON schema the output is required to conform to","Describing the required fields in the prompt in careful detail"],c:[2],e:"A supplied schema constrains the output as it is generated. Describing the shape or checking afterwards both allow malformed output to be produced first.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Which tool should be used to locate document templates by filename pattern across the repository?",o:["A file-reading tool applied to each directory","A shell command listing every file in the tree","A filename-matching tool such as glob","A content-search tool such as grep"],c:[2],e:"Filename patterns are what glob is for; grep searches contents, and reading or listing everything is far more expensive than a targeted match.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An extraction script needs a small, precise change to one function. Which editing approach fits best?",o:["A targeted edit to the specific section that needs changing","Rewriting the whole file so its contents are known to be consistent","Asking for the change as a description for the engineer to apply","Appending the corrected function and leaving the original in place"],c:[0],e:"A targeted edit is the least invasive change that achieves the result; a full rewrite risks disturbing code that was already correct.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Extraction runs overnight against tens of thousands of documents. What bounds when results are available?",o:["The rate limit that applies to synchronous requests during the working day","The size of the context window used for each individual document","The batch's processing window, within which submitted work completes or expires","The number of parallel workers the pipeline is configured with"],c:[2],e:"Asynchronous batch work is bounded by its processing window; requests not completed inside it expire rather than running indefinitely.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Some documents in an overnight batch expire without being processed. What is the appropriate handling?",o:["Treat the whole batch as having failed and resubmit all of it","Switch the entire workload to synchronous processing instead","Identify the unprocessed items and resubmit them in a later batch","Accept the gap, since expired items are not charged for"],c:[2],e:"Expired items are identifiable individually, so resubmitting just those recovers the work without reprocessing what already succeeded.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The database-write tool should refuse any record whose totals do not reconcile. Where does that check belong?",o:["Inside the tool, so an unreconciled record cannot be written at all","In a report reviewed the following morning by the finance team","In the prompt, instructing the model to check before it writes","In the tool description, noting that totals are expected to reconcile"],c:[0],e:"A hard data-integrity rule belongs where it cannot be bypassed; prompt guidance and later review both allow the bad write to happen.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two controls should the write tool itself enforce, rather than document? (Select 2)",o:["An idempotency key so a retried write cannot duplicate a row","A description of the fields the downstream table expects","An atomic write so a partial record never lands in the table","A note that writes cannot be undone once they have been made"],c:[0,2],e:"Idempotency and atomicity are behaviours the tool must guarantee in code. The other two are worth stating in the description, but a description cannot enforce anything.",sc:"extract"},
  ]
});
